<img width="400" height="600" src="https://foolproofliving.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/09/vegetarian-stuffed-grape-leaves-400x600.jpg" class="webfeedsFeaturedVisual wp-post-image" alt="A person is squeezing lemon juice over vegetarian stuffed grape leaves arranged on a plate from the front view." style="display: block; margin-bottom: 5px; clear:both;max-width: 100%;" link_thumbnail="" decoding="async" srcset="https://foolproofliving.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/09/vegetarian-stuffed-grape-leaves-400x600.jpg 400w, https://foolproofliving.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/09/vegetarian-stuffed-grape-leaves-768x1151.jpg 768w, https://foolproofliving.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/09/vegetarian-stuffed-grape-leaves-1025x1536.jpg 1025w, https://foolproofliving.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/09/vegetarian-stuffed-grape-leaves-150x225.jpg 150w, https://foolproofliving.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/09/vegetarian-stuffed-grape-leaves.jpg 1200w" sizes="(max-width: 400px) 100vw, 400px" /><div class="block-personal-note cwp-inner cwp-large has-background has-senary-background-color">

<figure class="wp-block-image alignright is-resized is-style-rounded"><img decoding="async" width="600" height="600" data-pin-nopin="true" src="https://foolproofliving.com/wp-content/uploads/2025/11/aysegul.jpg" alt="Aysegul Sanford from the front view." class="wp-image-84391" style="width:200px;height:200px" srcset="https://foolproofliving.com/wp-content/uploads/2025/11/aysegul.jpg 600w, https://foolproofliving.com/wp-content/uploads/2025/11/aysegul-400x400.jpg 400w, https://foolproofliving.com/wp-content/uploads/2025/11/aysegul-150x150.jpg 150w, https://foolproofliving.com/wp-content/uploads/2025/11/aysegul-500x500.jpg 500w, https://foolproofliving.com/wp-content/uploads/2025/11/aysegul-300x300.jpg 300w, https://foolproofliving.com/wp-content/uploads/2025/11/aysegul-96x96.jpg 96w, https://foolproofliving.com/wp-content/uploads/2025/11/aysegul-378x378.jpg 378w, https://foolproofliving.com/wp-content/uploads/2025/11/aysegul-276x276.jpg 276w" sizes="(max-width: 600px) 100vw, 600px" data-pin-media="https://foolproofliving.com/wp-content/uploads/2025/11/aysegul.jpg"></figure>



<div class="wp-block-group is-layout-constrained wp-block-group-is-layout-constrained">
<p class="wp-block-paragraph">My mother&rsquo;s version of this recipe lives on a torn piece of paper. I recently found it tucked away in an old cookbook and made it exactly as she wrote it, then adjusted it slightly with the ingredients I have here in the US to achieve the same taste.</p>
</div>



<p class="wp-block-paragraph">I have already shared her <a href="https://foolproofliving.com/stuffed-grape-leaves-recipe/">stuffed grape leaves with meat and rice</a>. This is the vegetarian version we made when she wanted an appetizer she could serve cold or at room temperature, and that is not a coincidence.</p>



<p class="wp-block-paragraph">This vegetarian stuffed grape leaves recipe, or as we call it, <em>zeytinyagli dolma</em>, belongs to a category of dishes we call &ldquo;zeytinya&#287;l&#305;&rdquo;, cooked in olive oil and mostly served cool rather than hot off the stove. Lebanese, Greek, and Egyptian cuisines have their versions too. They are close cousins with their own names; they differ slightly but follow the same principle.</p>

</div>


<figure class="wp-block-image aligncenter size-full"><img decoding="async" width="1200" height="801" data-pin-nopin="true" src="https://foolproofliving.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/09/vegan-stuffed-grape-leaves.jpg" alt="Person squeezing lemon juice over vegetarian stuffed grape leaves (dolmas) on a plate." class="wp-image-89129" srcset="https://foolproofliving.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/09/vegan-stuffed-grape-leaves.jpg 1200w, https://foolproofliving.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/09/vegan-stuffed-grape-leaves-400x267.jpg 400w, https://foolproofliving.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/09/vegan-stuffed-grape-leaves-768x513.jpg 768w, https://foolproofliving.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/09/vegan-stuffed-grape-leaves-150x100.jpg 150w" sizes="(max-width: 1200px) 100vw, 1200px" data-pin-media="https://foolproofliving.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/09/vegan-stuffed-grape-leaves.jpg"></figure>



<div class="wp-block-group is-layout-constrained wp-block-group-is-layout-constrained">
<h2 id="h-a-few-ingredient-notes" class="wp-block-heading">A Few Ingredient Notes</h2>



<p class="wp-block-paragraph">You can find the full list of ingredients with exact measurements in the recipe card. Below are my notes on the ingredients that need a bit more explanation to help you succeed, even if it is your first time making it.&nbsp;</p>



<p class="wp-block-paragraph">Anything not mentioned is pretty straightforward.&nbsp;</p>



<figure class="wp-block-image aligncenter size-large"><img decoding="async" width="1200" height="800" data-pin-nopin="true" src="https://foolproofliving.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/09/vegetarian-stuffed-grape-leaves-Ingredient-1200x800.jpg" alt="Ingredients for vegetarian stuffed grape leaves with text on each ingredient from the top view." class="wp-image-89151" srcset="https://foolproofliving.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/09/vegetarian-stuffed-grape-leaves-Ingredient-1200x800.jpg 1200w, https://foolproofliving.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/09/vegetarian-stuffed-grape-leaves-Ingredient-400x267.jpg 400w, https://foolproofliving.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/09/vegetarian-stuffed-grape-leaves-Ingredient-768x512.jpg 768w, https://foolproofliving.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/09/vegetarian-stuffed-grape-leaves-Ingredient-150x100.jpg 150w" sizes="(max-width: 1200px) 100vw, 1200px" data-pin-media="https://foolproofliving.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/09/vegetarian-stuffed-grape-leaves-Ingredient.jpg"></figure>



<p class="wp-block-paragraph"><strong>Jarred grape leaves: </strong>I tested this recipe with three brands, and they all worked well. The main difference was the brine&rsquo;s saltiness. My favorite was Orlando, which most big grocery stores carry, but it is also available <a href="https://link.amazon/B02T1UGZS" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer noopener nofollow">online</a>.</p>



<figure class="wp-block-image aligncenter size-full"><img decoding="async" width="1200" height="684" src="https://foolproofliving.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/09/different-brands-grape-leaves.jpg" alt="Three jars of grape leaves with different brands." class="wp-image-89152" srcset="https://foolproofliving.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/09/different-brands-grape-leaves.jpg 1200w, https://foolproofliving.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/09/different-brands-grape-leaves-400x228.jpg 400w, https://foolproofliving.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/09/different-brands-grape-leaves-768x438.jpg 768w, https://foolproofliving.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/09/different-brands-grape-leaves-150x86.jpg 150w" sizes="(max-width: 1200px) 100vw, 1200px" data-pin-media="https://foolproofliving.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/09/different-brands-grape-leaves.jpg"></figure>



<p class="wp-block-paragraph">No matter the brand, the important thing is to check the saltiness. Taste a torn leaf. If it is too salty for your liking, rinse it under tap water. Some people even recommend soaking them, but truth be told, none of the brands I tried were salty enough to need soaking.</p>



<p class="wp-block-paragraph"><strong>Rice: </strong>This recipe is traditionally made with a short-grain rice variety called Baldo, which can be hard to find here in the US. The closest substitute in taste and texture I found is <a href="https://link.amazon/B09XSk4Ho" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer noopener nofollow">Arborio</a>. It expands the same way and makes a substantial filling, thanks to its high starch content.</p>



<p class="wp-block-paragraph">While I strongly recommend short-grain rice, you can use long-grain (enriched) rice if that is all you have. However, keep in mind the cooking time may be slightly shorter, and the dolmas will not puff up quite as much.</p>


<div class="block-tip cwp-inner is-style-border has-background has-senary-background-color">

<p class="wp-block-paragraph"><strong>Should I rinse the rice before adding the filling? </strong>I know some people prefer not to wash their rice before cooking, but for this recipe I recommend rinsing it to remove the excess starch.</p>

</div></div>



<div class="wp-block-group is-layout-constrained wp-block-group-is-layout-constrained">
<h2 id="h-how-to-make-vegetarian-stuffed-grape-leaves" class="wp-block-heading">How To Make Vegetarian Stuffed Grape Leaves</h2>



<p class="wp-block-paragraph">No matter how you put it, rolling 60-65 grape leaves is a labor of love, the same way my mother turned it into an afternoon activity with her neighbors instead of a chore. Here is how we do it:</p>



<figure class="wp-block-image aligncenter size-full"><img decoding="async" width="1200" height="744" data-pin-nopin="true" src="https://foolproofliving.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/09/soaking-currants-sauteing-pine-nuts.jpg" alt="Two images side by side showing soaking currants and sauteeing of pine nuts in a skillet." class="wp-image-89130" srcset="https://foolproofliving.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/09/soaking-currants-sauteing-pine-nuts.jpg 1200w, https://foolproofliving.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/09/soaking-currants-sauteing-pine-nuts-400x248.jpg 400w, https://foolproofliving.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/09/soaking-currants-sauteing-pine-nuts-768x476.jpg 768w, https://foolproofliving.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/09/soaking-currants-sauteing-pine-nuts-150x93.jpg 150w" sizes="(max-width: 1200px) 100vw, 1200px" data-pin-media="https://foolproofliving.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/09/soaking-currants-sauteing-pine-nuts.jpg"></figure>



<p class="wp-block-paragraph"><strong>Step 1 &ndash;</strong> Place the currants in a bowl, pour hot water over them, and let them soak while you work on the rest of the ingredients.</p>



<p class="wp-block-paragraph"><strong>Step 2 &ndash;</strong> Heat a large skillet over medium heat, add the pine nuts, and saut&eacute; for 3 minutes, until lightly browned. This happens quickly, so watch closely and keep stirring.</p>



<figure class="wp-block-image aligncenter size-full"><img decoding="async" width="1200" height="744" data-pin-nopin="true" src="https://foolproofliving.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/09/cooking-pine-nuts-onions-in-olive-oil.jpg" alt="Pine nuts and onions cooking in olive oil in a skillet from the top view." class="wp-image-89131" srcset="https://foolproofliving.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/09/cooking-pine-nuts-onions-in-olive-oil.jpg 1200w, https://foolproofliving.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/09/cooking-pine-nuts-onions-in-olive-oil-400x248.jpg 400w, https://foolproofliving.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/09/cooking-pine-nuts-onions-in-olive-oil-768x476.jpg 768w, https://foolproofliving.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/09/cooking-pine-nuts-onions-in-olive-oil-150x93.jpg 150w" sizes="(max-width: 1200px) 100vw, 1200px" data-pin-media="https://foolproofliving.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/09/cooking-pine-nuts-onions-in-olive-oil.jpg"></figure>



<p class="wp-block-paragraph"><strong>Step 3 &ndash; </strong>Add the olive oil and cook for 3-4 minutes. As it heats, the pine nuts will continue to brown. Stir often to prevent them from burning.</p>



<p class="wp-block-paragraph"><strong>Step 4 &ndash;</strong> Stir in the onion and cook for 6-7 minutes, or until lightly browned. As they cook, the onions will be swimming in the oil, and that is intentional. The goal here is to flavor the oil so it infuses the rice as it cooks.</p>



<figure class="wp-block-image aligncenter size-full"><img decoding="async" width="1200" height="744" data-pin-nopin="true" src="https://foolproofliving.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/09/adding-spices-cooking-filling.jpg" alt="Spices, mint, sugar and currants added in and cooking in the skillet." class="wp-image-89132" srcset="https://foolproofliving.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/09/adding-spices-cooking-filling.jpg 1200w, https://foolproofliving.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/09/adding-spices-cooking-filling-400x248.jpg 400w, https://foolproofliving.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/09/adding-spices-cooking-filling-768x476.jpg 768w, https://foolproofliving.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/09/adding-spices-cooking-filling-150x93.jpg 150w" sizes="(max-width: 1200px) 100vw, 1200px" data-pin-media="https://foolproofliving.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/09/adding-spices-cooking-filling.jpg"></figure>



<p class="wp-block-paragraph"><strong>Step 5 &ndash; </strong>Add the spices and cook, stirring constantly, until combined.</p>



<p class="wp-block-paragraph"><strong>Step 6 &ndash;</strong> Stir in the soaked currants and their liquid.</p>



<figure class="wp-block-image aligncenter size-full"><img decoding="async" width="1200" height="744" data-pin-nopin="true" src="https://foolproofliving.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/09/filling-before-and-after-cooked.jpg" alt="A collage of images showing rice added into the skillet before and after it is cooked." class="wp-image-89133" srcset="https://foolproofliving.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/09/filling-before-and-after-cooked.jpg 1200w, https://foolproofliving.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/09/filling-before-and-after-cooked-400x248.jpg 400w, https://foolproofliving.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/09/filling-before-and-after-cooked-768x476.jpg 768w, https://foolproofliving.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/09/filling-before-and-after-cooked-150x93.jpg 150w" sizes="(max-width: 1200px) 100vw, 1200px" data-pin-media="https://foolproofliving.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/09/filling-before-and-after-cooked.jpg"></figure>



<p class="wp-block-paragraph"><strong>Step 7 &ndash;</strong> Add the rice and &frac12; cup of hot tap water. Put the lid on, bring to a boil, then reduce the heat to medium-low. Simmer until all of the liquid is absorbed, about 15 minutes.</p>


<div class="block-tip cwp-inner is-style-border has-background has-senary-background-color">

<p class="wp-block-paragraph"><strong>At this stage, the rice will not be fully cooked, and this is normal.</strong> Remember, we will continue cooking the filling after we stuff the leaves.</p>

</div>


<p class="wp-block-paragraph"><strong>Step 8 &ndash; </strong>Turn the heat off and let the filling cool for an hour. Stir in the parsley and dill to combine.</p>



<figure class="wp-block-image aligncenter size-full"><img decoding="async" width="1200" height="744" data-pin-nopin="true" src="https://foolproofliving.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/09/preparing-grape-leaves-before-rolling.jpg" alt="Person is cutting the stems of a bunch of grape leaves and then lining the bottom of the pot with leaves." class="wp-image-89134" srcset="https://foolproofliving.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/09/preparing-grape-leaves-before-rolling.jpg 1200w, https://foolproofliving.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/09/preparing-grape-leaves-before-rolling-400x248.jpg 400w, https://foolproofliving.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/09/preparing-grape-leaves-before-rolling-768x476.jpg 768w, https://foolproofliving.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/09/preparing-grape-leaves-before-rolling-150x93.jpg 150w" sizes="(max-width: 1200px) 100vw, 1200px" data-pin-media="https://foolproofliving.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/09/preparing-grape-leaves-before-rolling.jpg"></figure>



<p class="wp-block-paragraph"><strong>Step 9 &ndash; </strong>While the filling cools, remove the grape leaves from the jar and rinse them in a colander under warm tap water for 1 minute.</p>



<p class="wp-block-paragraph">You can remove the stems one by one, but I like to use my mom&rsquo;s shortcut instead. Align the stems of a handful of leaves (7 to 8 stems) and trim them all at once with kitchen shears. Repeat for the remaining leaves.</p>



<p class="wp-block-paragraph">Place them in a colander set in a bowl and let them drain.</p>



<p class="wp-block-paragraph"><strong>Step 10 &ndash;</strong> Set aside a handful of torn leaves. Brush the bottom of a 10-inch pot (with a tight-fitting lid) with olive oil and line the bottom with those leaves. </p>



<p class="wp-block-paragraph">They act like a barrier between the pot and the heat and prevent the bottom layer from burning.</p>


<div class="block-tip cwp-inner is-style-border has-background has-senary-background-color">

<p class="wp-block-paragraph"><strong>Should I line the bottom of the pot with vegetables? </strong>You do not have to.</p>



<p class="wp-block-paragraph">If you are unfamiliar, some versions of this meatless stuffed grape leaves recipe add a layer of sliced potatoes on top of the leaves lining the pot. The idea is to protect the bottom layer of dolmas and absorb any excess liquid.&nbsp;</p>



<p class="wp-block-paragraph">After testing this recipe several times, I did not feel the need to line the bottom with anything else. Since the rice is partially cooked and the stuffed grape leaves cook over low heat for a relatively short time, lining the bottom with leaves alone was sufficient.</p>

</div>


<figure class="wp-block-image aligncenter size-full"><img decoding="async" width="1200" height="744" data-pin-nopin="true" src="https://foolproofliving.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/09/rolling-vegan-stuffed-grape-leaves.jpg" alt="Person showing how to fill and roll stuffed grapes with four images showing each step." class="wp-image-89135" srcset="https://foolproofliving.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/09/rolling-vegan-stuffed-grape-leaves.jpg 1200w, https://foolproofliving.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/09/rolling-vegan-stuffed-grape-leaves-400x248.jpg 400w, https://foolproofliving.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/09/rolling-vegan-stuffed-grape-leaves-768x476.jpg 768w, https://foolproofliving.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/09/rolling-vegan-stuffed-grape-leaves-150x93.jpg 150w" sizes="(max-width: 1200px) 100vw, 1200px" data-pin-media="https://foolproofliving.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/09/rolling-vegan-stuffed-grape-leaves.jpg"></figure>



<p class="wp-block-paragraph"><strong>Step 11 &ndash;</strong> To roll, place a grape leaf shiny side down on a cutting board. Place a small amount of filling in the center, near the stem end. </p>



<p class="wp-block-paragraph">I use about 1 to 1 &frac12; teaspoons of filling for a 6-inch leaf. If yours is larger, you can go up to 2 teaspoons.</p>



<p class="wp-block-paragraph">Fold the sides over and roll from bottom to top. Place seam side down in the pot.&nbsp;</p>


<div class="block-tip cwp-inner is-style-border has-background has-senary-background-color">

<p class="wp-block-paragraph"><strong>A note on managing the leaf-to-filling ratio:</strong> A 16-ounce jar of grape leaves gives you about 70 leaves. Once you set aside the torn ones, you are left with 60-65 leaves to roll.</p>



<p class="wp-block-paragraph">The variable here is the leaf size, which is why ending up short on filling or extra is a real concern. </p>



<p class="wp-block-paragraph">My fix is to adjust how much filling you use as you get close to the end. Once I have about 10 to 12 leaves left, I divide the remaining filling into equal portions so I don&rsquo;t run out or end up with leftovers.</p>



<p class="wp-block-paragraph"><strong>A note on how tight to roll: </strong>This is more of a personal preference. In T&uuml;rkiye, some people insist that stuffed grape leaves should be thin as a pencil (using less than 1 teaspoon of filling per leaf). Others, depending on the region, prefer them thicker.&nbsp;</p>



<p class="wp-block-paragraph">I like mine on the thicker side, so every bite has a bit of currants and pine nuts.&nbsp;</p>



<p class="wp-block-paragraph">That said, one rule matters no matter which you prefer: <strong>do not roll it too tight.</strong> The rice still needs room to expand during the final cooking, and if you roll it too tightly, it could easily tear.</p>

</div>


<figure class="wp-block-image aligncenter size-full"><img decoding="async" width="1200" height="744" data-pin-nopin="true" src="https://foolproofliving.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/09/arranging-stuffed-grape-leaves-in-pot-with-lemons.jpg" alt="Person filling a pot with vegan stuffed grape leaves in a spiral pattern and adding slices on top." class="wp-image-89136" srcset="https://foolproofliving.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/09/arranging-stuffed-grape-leaves-in-pot-with-lemons.jpg 1200w, https://foolproofliving.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/09/arranging-stuffed-grape-leaves-in-pot-with-lemons-400x248.jpg 400w, https://foolproofliving.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/09/arranging-stuffed-grape-leaves-in-pot-with-lemons-768x476.jpg 768w, https://foolproofliving.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/09/arranging-stuffed-grape-leaves-in-pot-with-lemons-150x93.jpg 150w" sizes="(max-width: 1200px) 100vw, 1200px" data-pin-media="https://foolproofliving.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/09/arranging-stuffed-grape-leaves-in-pot-with-lemons.jpg"></figure>



<p class="wp-block-paragraph"><strong>Step 12 &ndash;</strong> Repeat the same process until you run out of leaves. Arrange them in a spiral pattern with about 30 to 32 dolmas per layer. Scatter lemon slices on top.&nbsp;</p>



<figure class="wp-block-image aligncenter size-full"><img decoding="async" width="1200" height="744" data-pin-nopin="true" src="https://foolproofliving.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/09/inverted-plate-over-dolmas.jpg" alt="An inverted plate is placed on top of the dolmas and someone is pouring on liquid in the pot from the top view." class="wp-image-89139" srcset="https://foolproofliving.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/09/inverted-plate-over-dolmas.jpg 1200w, https://foolproofliving.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/09/inverted-plate-over-dolmas-400x248.jpg 400w, https://foolproofliving.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/09/inverted-plate-over-dolmas-768x476.jpg 768w, https://foolproofliving.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/09/inverted-plate-over-dolmas-150x93.jpg 150w" sizes="(max-width: 1200px) 100vw, 1200px" data-pin-media="https://foolproofliving.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/09/inverted-plate-over-dolmas.jpg"></figure>



<p class="wp-block-paragraph"><strong>Step 13 &ndash;</strong> Place a small inverted plate over the stuffed grape leaves to keep them in place.</p>


<div class="block-tip cwp-inner is-style-border has-background has-senary-background-color">

<p class="wp-block-paragraph"><strong>Should you add lemon juice to the cooking liquid? </strong>It is a personal preference.&nbsp;</p>



<p class="wp-block-paragraph">While researching this recipe, I saw that Lebanese versions add lemon juice either in the filling or the cooking liquid.&nbsp;</p>



<p class="wp-block-paragraph">My mom never did, so I stuck to her version. Instead, she served it with plenty of lemon wedges so you can adjust it to your liking.</p>



<p class="wp-block-paragraph">Still, if you want yours with lemon juice, I would substitute &frac14; of the cooking liquid with lemon juice.</p>

</div>


<p class="wp-block-paragraph"><strong>Step 14 &ndash;</strong> Slowly pour water over the leaves, turn the heat to medium, cover, and bring to a boil. Then reduce the heat to low and simmer until most of the liquid is absorbed, about 30-35 minutes.</p>


<div class="block-tip cwp-inner is-style-border has-background has-senary-background-color">

<p class="wp-block-paragraph"><strong>Pro tip: </strong>Keep a close eye on it and check it a few times, especially towards the end, to ensure there is enough water in the pot to prevent the bottom layer from burning.</p>

</div>


<figure class="wp-block-image aligncenter size-full"><img decoding="async" width="1200" height="744" data-pin-nopin="true" src="https://foolproofliving.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/09/cooked-stuffed-grape-leaves-vegetarian.jpg" alt="Cooked vegetarian stuffed leaves with sliced lemons from the top view." class="wp-image-89140" srcset="https://foolproofliving.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/09/cooked-stuffed-grape-leaves-vegetarian.jpg 1200w, https://foolproofliving.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/09/cooked-stuffed-grape-leaves-vegetarian-400x248.jpg 400w, https://foolproofliving.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/09/cooked-stuffed-grape-leaves-vegetarian-768x476.jpg 768w, https://foolproofliving.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/09/cooked-stuffed-grape-leaves-vegetarian-150x93.jpg 150w" sizes="(max-width: 1200px) 100vw, 1200px" data-pin-media="https://foolproofliving.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/09/cooked-stuffed-grape-leaves-vegetarian.jpg"></figure>



<p class="wp-block-paragraph"><strong>Step 15 &ndash; </strong>Once the cooking time is up, turn off the heat and let it cool to room temperature, which takes about 4 to 5 hours. </p>



<p class="wp-block-paragraph">You can enjoy it right away with lemon wedges on the side, or do what my mom did and let it rest in the fridge overnight so the flavors can meld, then enjoy it cold the next day.</p>
</div>



<div class="wp-block-group is-layout-constrained wp-block-group-is-layout-constrained">
<h2 id="h-storage-and-freezing-instructions" class="wp-block-heading">Storage and Freezing Instructions</h2>



<p class="wp-block-paragraph"><strong>Storage:</strong> Once dolmas have cooled, arrange them carefully in an airtight container, rather than piling them in. A little care goes a long way here, because we want them to be snug so they won&rsquo;t fall apart when you go back for more.</p>



<p class="wp-block-paragraph"><strong>Freezing: </strong>You can freeze this dish, but if I am being honest, freshly cooked stuffed grape leaves taste better than the frozen-and-thawed version.&nbsp;</p>



<p class="wp-block-paragraph">That said, if you have to, I would recommend freezing them following the steps below:</p>



<ul class="wp-block-list">
<li>After you roll the grape leaves, arrange them in a single layer on a baking sheet instead of arranging them in the pot. Cover them tightly with plastic wrap to prevent freezer burn.</li>



<li>Flash freeze them for 3-4 hours or overnight. Once frozen, arrange them in a freezer-safe container. Cover it tightly with a lid and freeze for up to 3 months.</li>



<li>Thaw them in the fridge overnight. When you are ready to cook, arrange them in a pot and cook as the recipe says.</li>
</ul>



<p class="wp-block-paragraph">I do not recommend freezing them after they are cooked. Since the rice is already cooked once, it turns mushy rather than tender.</p>


