<div id="recipe-89090-instructions" class="wprm-recipe-instructions-container wprm-recipe-89090-instructions-container wprm-block-text-normal" data-recipe="89090"><h3 class="wprm-recipe-header wprm-recipe-instructions-header wprm-block-text-bold wprm-align-left wprm-header-decoration-none" style="">Instructions</h3><div class="wprm-recipe-instruction-group"><ul class="wprm-recipe-instructions"><li id="wprm-recipe-89090-step-0-0" class="wprm-recipe-instruction" style="list-style-type: decimal;"><div class="wprm-recipe-instruction-text" style="margin-bottom: 5px;">Place currants in a bowl and add hot water. Set it aside.</div></li><li id="wprm-recipe-89090-step-0-1" class="wprm-recipe-instruction" style="list-style-type: decimal;"><div class="wprm-recipe-instruction-text" style="margin-bottom: 5px;">Heat a large skillet over medium heat. Add pine nuts and cook, stirring frequently, until lightly browned, for 3 minutes.</div></li><li id="wprm-recipe-89090-step-0-2" class="wprm-recipe-instruction" style="list-style-type: decimal;"><div class="wprm-recipe-instruction-text" style="margin-bottom: 5px;">Add olive oil and let it cook for 3-4 minutes. As it warms, the pine nuts will continue to brown, and you will start seeing the oil bubble. Stir frequently to prevent them from burning.</div></li><li id="wprm-recipe-89090-step-0-3" class="wprm-recipe-instruction" style="list-style-type: decimal;"><div class="wprm-recipe-instruction-text" style="margin-bottom: 5px;">Add the onion and cook until softened, stirring often, and lightly browned, about 6-7 minutes.</div></li><li id="wprm-recipe-89090-step-0-4" class="wprm-recipe-instruction" style="list-style-type: decimal;"><div class="wprm-recipe-instruction-text" style="margin-bottom: 5px;">Stir in the allspice, ground cloves, ground cinnamon, dried mint, sugar, kosher salt, and black pepper. Cook, stirring constantly, until everything is mixed.</div></li><li id="wprm-recipe-89090-step-0-5" class="wprm-recipe-instruction" style="list-style-type: decimal;"><div class="wprm-recipe-instruction-text" style="margin-bottom: 5px;">Add the soaked currants with their liquid, stirring to combine.</div></li><li id="wprm-recipe-89090-step-0-6" class="wprm-recipe-instruction" style="list-style-type: decimal;"><div class="wprm-recipe-instruction-text" style="margin-bottom: 5px;">Stir in the rice along with &frac12; cup hot (tap) water. Bring to a boil, put the lid on, reduce the heat to medium-low, and simmer for 15 minutes, or until all the liquid is absorbed. At this point, we are not fully cooking the rice; it should be about 75% cooked.</div></li><li id="wprm-recipe-89090-step-0-7" class="wprm-recipe-instruction" style="list-style-type: decimal;"><div class="wprm-recipe-instruction-text" style="margin-bottom: 5px;">Turn the heat off and let it cool (with the lid on) for 1 hour.</div></li><li id="wprm-recipe-89090-step-0-8" class="wprm-recipe-instruction" style="list-style-type: decimal;"><div class="wprm-recipe-instruction-text" style="margin-bottom: 5px;">Add the parsley and dill and stir to combine.</div></li><li id="wprm-recipe-89090-step-0-9" class="wprm-recipe-instruction" style="list-style-type: decimal;"><div class="wprm-recipe-instruction-text" style="margin-bottom: 5px;">While it cools, remove the grape leaves from the jar, place them in a colander, and rinse them under warm tap water for 1 minute.</div></li><li id="wprm-recipe-89090-step-0-10" class="wprm-recipe-instruction" style="list-style-type: decimal;"><div class="wprm-recipe-instruction-text" style="margin-bottom: 5px;">Working in groups of 7 to 8 leaves, align the stems and trim them all at once with kitchen scissors. Repeat until you run out of leaves.</div></li><li id="wprm-recipe-89090-step-0-11" class="wprm-recipe-instruction" style="list-style-type: decimal;"><div class="wprm-recipe-instruction-text" style="margin-bottom: 5px;">Pick out a few of the torn leaves and set them aside.</div></li><li id="wprm-recipe-89090-step-0-12" class="wprm-recipe-instruction" style="list-style-type: decimal;"><div class="wprm-recipe-instruction-text" style="margin-bottom: 5px;">Drizzle the bottom of a large pot with a tablespoon of olive oil, then line the bottom with the torn leaves.</div></li><li id="wprm-recipe-89090-step-0-13" class="wprm-recipe-instruction" style="list-style-type: decimal;"><div class="wprm-recipe-instruction-text" style="margin-bottom: 5px;">To roll, lay one grape leaf shiny side down on a cutting board. Place 1 to 1&frac12; teaspoons of filling in the center, near the stem end, and shape it into a 1&frac12;- to 2-inch cylinder. Fold the sides over the filling and roll from bottom to top. The finished roll should be approximately 2 to 2&frac12; inches long. Place seam side down in the pot.</div></li><li id="wprm-recipe-89090-step-0-14" class="wprm-recipe-instruction" style="list-style-type: decimal;"><div class="wprm-recipe-instruction-text" style="margin-bottom: 5px;">Repeat the process with the remaining grape leaves, filling the pot in a spiral pattern. You should have about 30 to 32 dolmas per layer in a 10 or 12-inch pot, which should give you 2 layers in total. If needed, you can do a third layer.</div></li><li id="wprm-recipe-89090-step-0-15" class="wprm-recipe-instruction" style="list-style-type: decimal;"><div class="wprm-recipe-instruction-text" style="margin-bottom: 5px;">Scatter the sliced lemon over the top and place a small inverted plate directly over the dolmas to keep them in place.</div></li><li id="wprm-recipe-89090-step-0-16" class="wprm-recipe-instruction" style="list-style-type: decimal;"><div class="wprm-recipe-instruction-text" style="margin-bottom: 5px;">Pour hot (tap) water over the dolmas, turn the heat to medium, put the lid on, bring to a boil. Once boiled, turn the heat down to low and simmer for about 30-35 minutes, or until most of the liquid is absorbed.</div></li><li id="wprm-recipe-89090-step-0-17" class="wprm-recipe-instruction" style="list-style-type: decimal;"><div class="wprm-recipe-instruction-text" style="margin-bottom: 5px;"><span style="display: block;">Turn the heat off and let it cool to room temperature, which takes about 4 to 5 hours. You can serve it then, or better yet, let it rest in the fridge overnight and serve it cold the next day.</span></div></li></ul></div></div>
